// Reading and writing JSON as the declared types a program already has.
//
//	struct Pokemon {
//	    id   int
//	    name string
//	    #[json.identifier("base_experience")]
//	    baseExperience option[int]
//	}
//
//	match json.decode[Pokemon](text) {
//	    ok(var p) { … }
//	    failed(var f) { … }   // f.kind is decode
//	}
//
// `decode` answers an `async.Result`, the value or the failure that kept it
// from arriving, as a request does: text that does not match the type is a
// value a screen renders, not a raise. `encode` writes a value back.
//
// **A type is its own schema.** A struct is an object whose keys are its
// fields, an `option` a key that may be absent or `null`, a `list` an array, a
// `map[string, V]` an object of any keys, an enum carrying nothing a string
// naming its member, `datetime` and `time.date` RFC 3339 strings. A key the
// type does not name is ignored; a field that is not an option and has no key
// is a decode failure, and so is a value of the wrong kind. Writing leaves an
// absent option's key out. A unit, a function, a component, a payload enum and
// a map keyed by anything but a string have no JSON, and are refused at the
// `decode` or `encode` that reaches them.
//
// **A name on the wire is a fact about the format**, so it is this package's
// mark: `#[json.identifier("base_experience")]` on a struct field or an enum
// member, and no mark means the SNGL name. The field keeps its own name
// everywhere else, on every host.
//
// The type must be known where `decode` or `encode` is called: a call inside
// a generic function whose type parameter is the type is refused.
